{
  "teamId": 121,
  "year": 2025,
  "narrative": "The 2025 New York Mets opened their season with a burst of electricity, riding the thunder of Pete Alonso, Francisco Lindor, and a rejuvenated supporting cast through a promising April. Alonso announced his intentions early with a three-run bomb on Opening Weekend, and Jesse Winker's improbable clutch triple announced that this group had the firepower to compete. For much of the first half, the Mets looked like genuine contenders, with timely hitting and a lineup that could erupt at any moment.\n\nThe cracks, however, began to show in the bullpen. A.J. Minter's late-game collapse on April 25 counterbalanced Mark Vientos's heroic triple, and by the time June arrived, the relievers were becoming a liability the offense could not always overcome. Huascar Brazobán surrendering a go-ahead homer to Max Muncy in June and Reed Garrett's brutal multi-run double to Marcell Ozuna in mid-June exemplified the club's fatal flaw — a back-end bullpen that gave back leads almost as fast as the lineup created them.\n\nThe second half brought flickers of hope alongside gut-punch defeats. Jeff McNeil's Independence Day homer and Mark Vientos's breakout double in July kept the Mets within striking distance, but Edwin Díaz — the franchise's closer cornerstone — suffered through uncharacteristic meltdowns in August and September that proved devastating. Reed Garrett's concession of a Michael Harris II grand slam and Tyler Rogers surrendering an inside-the-park homer late in September symbolized a team that simply could not protect leads when it mattered most.\n\nJuan Soto delivered on his massive contract in stretches, most memorably with a grand slam on September 1st and a clutch single late in the month, but it was never quite enough to compensate for the bullpen's chronic failures. The Mets finished 83-79, a winning record that nonetheless left them on the outside of the playoffs looking in — a season of genuine talent undermined by an inability to close out games, a frustrating reminder that in baseball, the final three outs can define everything.",
